    <script>
      (function() {
        var rail = document.getElementById('workflowRail');
        var dotsWrap = document.getElementById('workflowRailDots');
        if (!rail || !dotsWrap || !rail.children.length) return;
        var idx = 0, paused = false, lastAuto = 0, pausedTimer = null;
        function perView() {
          var w = rail.clientWidth;
          return w > 1000 ? 4 : w > 720 ? 3 : w > 480 ? 2 : 1;
        }
        function maxIdx() { return Math.max(0, rail.children.length - perView()); }
        function updateDots() {
          Array.prototype.forEach.call(dotsWrap.children, function(d, j) { d.classList.toggle('active', j === idx); });
        }
        function buildDots() {
          dotsWrap.innerHTML = '';
          for (var i = 0; i <= maxIdx(); i++) {
            (function(i) {
              var b = document.createElement('button');
              b.className = 'workflow-rail-dot' + (i === idx ? ' active' : '');
              b.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Go to slide ' + (i + 1));
              b.addEventListener('click', function() { go(i); });
              dotsWrap.appendChild(b);
            })(i);
          }
        }
        function go(i) {
          idx = Math.max(0, Math.min(i, maxIdx()));
          lastAuto = Date.now();
          rail.scrollTo({ left: idx * (rail.clientWidth / perView()), behavior: 'smooth' });
          updateDots();
        }
        buildDots();
        rail.addEventListener('mouseenter', function() { paused = true; });
        rail.addEventListener('mouseleave', function() { paused = false; });
        rail.addEventListener('focusin', function() { paused = true; });
        rail.addEventListener('focusout', function() { paused = false; });
        rail.addEventListener('scroll', function() {
          if (Date.now() - lastAuto > 600) {
            idx = Math.max(0, Math.min(Math.round(rail.scrollLeft / (rail.clientWidth / perView())), maxIdx()));
            updateDots();
            paused = true;
            clearTimeout(pausedTimer);
            pausedTimer = setTimeout(function() { paused = false; }, 4000);
          }
        });
        setInterval(function() { if (!paused) go(idx + 1); }, 3500);
        window.addEventListener('resize', function() { buildDots(); go(idx); });
      })();
    </script>

    <!-- Verified price comparison — the money-term section -->
    <section class="compare-section" id="compare">
      <div class="compare-inner">
        <div class="section-header">
          <h2>Cheapest SERP API Pricing &mdash; $0.03 per 1,000 Calls</h2>
          <p>Six named rivals, priced from each provider's published rates. <span class="verified-stamp">Checked 10 September 2026</span></p>
        </div>

        <p class="table-hint">Swipe the table sideways to see every column.</p>
        <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="SERP API price comparison table, scrollable">
          <table class="comparison-table">
            <caption class="visually-hidden">SERP API price comparison: entry price, cheapest published price, free tier and AI Overview handling across seven providers</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Provider</th>
                <th scope="col">Entry price /1K</th>
                <th scope="col">Cheapest published /1K</th>
                <th scope="col">Free tier</th>
                <th scope="col">AI Overviews</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="highlight-row">
                <th scope="row">Serpent API <span class="row-note">web, all engines</span></th>
                <td class="price-good">$0.60</td>
                <td class="price-good">$0.03 <span class="row-note">qualifying $1,000 deposit, per Web unit</span></td>
                <td>Free to start</td>
                <td>One flag, same call, no surcharge</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Scrapingdog</th>
                <td>$1.00 <span class="row-note">LITE, $40/mo</span></td>
                <td class="price-bad">$0.14 <span class="row-note">$30,000 plan</span></td>
                <td>200 credits <span class="row-note">~40 Google searches</span></td>
                <td>Separate request</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Serper</th>
                <td>$1.00</td>
                <td class="price-bad">$0.30 <span class="row-note">12.5M credits, expire in 6 months</span></td>
                <td>2,500 queries</td>
                <td>No endpoint published</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">DataForSEO</th>
                <td>$0.60</td>
                <td class="price-bad">$0.60 <span class="row-note">~5-min queue; $2.00 synchronous</span></td>
                <td>~$1 trial credit</td>
                <td>Doubles the task price</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Oxylabs</th>
                <td>$1.00</td>
                <td class="price-bad">$0.50 <span class="row-note">quote-only Custom+ tier; Google billed at 2&times; base</span></td>
                <td>2,000 results</td>
                <td>No published support</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Bright Data</th>
                <td>$1.50</td>
                <td class="price-bad">$1.30 <span class="row-note">$499/mo Scale plan</span></td>
                <td>5,000/mo</td>
                <td>Not stated</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">SerpApi</th>
                <td class="price-bad">$25.00</td>
                <td class="price-bad">$1.96 <span class="row-note">$106,050 plan</span></td>
                <td>250/mo</td>
                <td>Sometimes a separate request</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="compare-sources">Rates read from each provider's published pricing on 10 September 2026:
          <a href="https://serpapi.com/pricing" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">SerpApi</a> &middot;
          <a href="https://dataforseo.com/pricing/google-serp/google-organic-serp-api" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">DataForSEO</a> &middot;
          <a href="https://brightdata.com/pricing/serp" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Bright Data</a> &middot;
          <a href="https://oxylabs.io/products/scraper-api/serp" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Oxylabs</a> &middot;
          <a href="https://www.scrapingdog.com/pricing/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Scrapingdog</a>.
          <a href="https://serper.dev/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Serper's homepage</a> publicly listed its full pack ladder and six-month validity when checked 6 October 2026; see our <a href="/blog/serper-pricing-credits-explained">Serper pricing breakdown</a>.
          Oxylabs prices its plan tiles in a rotating carousel, so a single page view shows only part of the ladder; the $0.50 above is the Google rate on its quote-only Custom+ tier, and the cheapest rate on a tier with a published monthly price is $0.60 on the $999 Business plan.
          &ldquo;Entry&rdquo; is the lowest paid plan. &ldquo;Cheapest published&rdquo; is the best rate a vendor lists, whatever it costs to reach. &ldquo;No published support&rdquo; and &ldquo;not stated&rdquo; mean we found nothing about AI Overviews on that vendor's public pages on the date above &mdash; not that the feature is absent.
        </p>

        <div class="compare-prose">
          <h3>Read the unit, not just the number</h3>
          <p>Two vendors can quote the same price for very different things. DataForSEO states that <a href="https://dataforseo.com/pricing/google-serp/google-organic-serp-api" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">one SERP contains 10 search results</a>, and their cost formula multiplies the price by <code>depth / 10</code>. So 100 Google results cost $0.006 on their $0.60 queue &mdash; and that queue is asynchronous, typically about five minutes. One synchronous Serpent Deep Search call returns up to 100 organic results across 10 pages for $0.001 at the Scale rate or $0.0003 at Enterprise, because Deep Search bills one unit per page. Entry tier against entry tier we are level with them at $0.60; it is the discount ladder that opens the gap.</p>
          <p>Serper is the closest published rate above ours. It brands itself the cheapest Google Search API, and its pack ladder runs from $1.00 per 1,000 down to $0.30 per 1,000 once you buy 12.5&nbsp;million credits &mdash; $3,750 up front, expiring after six months. Our Scale Web rate is $0.10/1K after one $500 deposit; Enterprise is $0.03/1K after one $1,000 deposit. Deposited credit does not expire. Refunded or charged-back amounts don't count toward your tier.</p>

          <h3>Entry price matters more than the floor</h3>
          <p>Most teams never reach the volume that unlocks a vendor's best rate, which makes the entry price the number you actually pay. Ours is $0.60 per 1,000 calls, and it drops to $0.30 after a one-time $100 deposit. SerpApi's entry plan is $25 per month for 1,000 searches. That is <strong>42&times;</strong> our entry rate comparing like with like, and <strong>833&times;</strong> at our Enterprise rate.</p>

          <h3>A deposit, not a subscription</h3>
          <p>You are not buying a seat or a monthly plan. There is no monthly minimum spend. The first paid deposit is at least $10, and further top-ups have a $100 minimum after lifetime deposits reach $50. New credit follows the tier earned by your largest qualifying single deposit, old credit keeps its original schedule, and deposited credit does not expire. Refunded or charged-back amounts don't count toward your tier. Requests we could not complete are refunded automatically; a 404 for a resource that does not exist is an answer and is charged. If you stop calling the API for three months, nothing is lost. Promotional credits are the one exception &mdash; those lapse 30 days after they are granted.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <!-- USP #2 made provable: what one call actually returns -->
    <section class="blocks-section" id="serp-blocks">
      <div class="blocks-inner">
        <div class="section-header">
          <h2>The Cheapest SERP API That Still Returns the Whole Page</h2>
          <p>Most cheap APIs return ten blue links. One Deep Search call on <code>engine=google</code> parses every block below, with these field names.</p>
        </div>

        <p class="table-hint">Swipe the table sideways to see every column.</p>
        <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="SERP blocks and JSON fields table, scrollable">
          <table class="comparison-table blocks-table">
            <caption class="visually-hidden">SERP blocks returned by a Google Deep Search call, their JSON field names, and how rival APIs handle them</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">SERP block</th>
                <th scope="col">JSON field</th>
                <th scope="col">How you get it</th>
                <th scope="col">Elsewhere</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">AI Overview</th>
                <td><code>results.aiOverview</code></td>
                <td>Add <code>include_aio=true</code> to a Deep Search call. No surcharge.</td>
                <td>SerpApi and Scrapingdog sometimes need a second request. DataForSEO doubles the task price when the async overview is fetched. Serper publishes no endpoint.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Featured snippet</th>
                <td><code>results.featuredSnippet</code></td>
                <td>Deep Search, parsed when present</td>
                <td>Standard across vendors</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">People Also Ask</th>
                <td><code>results.peopleAlsoAsk</code></td>
                <td>Deep Search. Question, answer and source.</td>
                <td>Standard across vendors</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Knowledge panel</th>
                <td><code>results.knowledgePanel</code></td>
                <td>Deep Search. Title, description, facts and links.</td>
                <td>Standard across vendors</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Local pack</th>
                <td><code>results.localPack</code></td>
                <td>Deep Search. Name, rating, reviews, category, address, hours.</td>
                <td>Serper bills a separate <code>/places</code> call</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Ads</th>
                <td><code>results.ads</code></td>
                <td>Deep Search. Split into <code>top</code>, <code>bottom</code> and <code>totalCount</code>.</td>
                <td>Standard across vendors</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Inline videos</th>
                <td><code>results.videos</code></td>
                <td>Deep Search, parsed when present</td>
                <td>Standard across vendors</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Sitelinks</th>
                <td><code>results.organic[].sitelinks</code></td>
                <td>Deep Search. Nested under the result that owns them.</td>
                <td>Standard across vendors</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Related searches</th>
                <td><code>results.relatedSearches</code></td>
                <td>Deep Search, parsed when present</td>
                <td>Standard across vendors</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Pixel position</th>
                <td><code>pixel_position</code>, <code>pixel_box</code></td>
                <td>Add <code>pixel_position=true</code>. Beta, desktop, paid tiers. No surcharge.</td>
                <td>DataForSEO doubles the task price for <code>calculate_rectangles</code>. Oxylabs publishes ordinal positions, not coordinates.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="compare-sources">Coverage varies by endpoint and engine &mdash; the table above describes Deep Search on <code>engine=google</code>. Quick Search on <code>engine=google</code> is organic-only and speed-optimized; Bing, Yahoo, DuckDuckGo and Brave each return their own subset on Quick and Deep alike. The <a href="/docs">API reference</a> lists which block each engine supports. Competitor behaviour read from published documentation on 20 August 2026.</p>

        <div class="compare-prose">
          <h3>Why the blocks are the whole point</h3>
          <p>Rank one is not the top of the page any more. An AI Overview, a local pack and four ads can push the first organic result below the fold. If your API only returns organic links, your rank report is describing a page your customer never saw.</p>
          <p>That is what <code>pixel_position</code> is for. Every item gets a y-coordinate measured from the top of a rendered 1366&times;768 desktop page, plus a <code>pixel_box</code> with the full bounding rectangle. You can tell a client that they rank third and sit 1,400 pixels down, which is a different conversation from &ldquo;you rank third.&rdquo;</p>

          <h3>Five engines, one response shape</h3>
          <p>Google, Bing, Yahoo, DuckDuckGo and Brave all return the same JSON shape. Switch with one <code>engine</code> parameter, and fields a given engine does not carry come back null or empty rather than changing structure. You write the parser once, so adding a second engine costs no extra engineering.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

